Title: setup-claude-agent.sh greps .env for a key that no longer lives there
Created: 2026-09-16
Author: claude

--- log ---
2026-09-16T13:31Z claude created

--- body ---
## Context

`scripts/setup-claude-agent.sh:75`:

```sh
grep 'ANTHROPIC_API_KEY' "$HARNESS/.env" | sudo tee /etc/claude-agent/env >/dev/null
```

This assumes `~/.claude/.env` holds literal `VAR=value` credential assignments.
It has not since the KEYS mechanism landed — the file's own header states "NO
SECRET MATERIAL LIVES HERE. This file names keys; it never holds them."

So the script was already wrong before 0939 touched anything: its grep matched
the `KEYS=` line by substring and wrote the whole *selector* line into
`/etc/claude-agent/env`, where the service would read it as a credential
assignment named KEYS. Removing `anthropic` from KEYS (2026-09-16) changes the
failure mode from "writes the wrong thing" to "writes nothing"; it does not
introduce the defect.

The grep-a-comment hazard is real and was hit during that edit: a first pass at
documenting the removal spelled the variable out in a `.env` comment, which the
grep would have piped verbatim into the service env file. The `.env` comment now
says explicitly why the token must not appear there — but a guard would be
better than a warning nobody reads.

Not fixed in the 0939 PR: how the agent should obtain its key is a design
question (read the keystore directly? a helper invoked at need?), and it is the
same question as the cross-runtime portability of the secrets mechanism.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] `setup-claude-agent.sh` obtains the key from the keystore, not by grepping `.env`
- [ ] It fails loudly when the key cannot be resolved, rather than writing an
      empty or malformed `/etc/claude-agent/env`
- [ ] A test covers the resolution path with a fake sentinel, never a real key
- [ ] No code path can write a `.env` comment line into a service env file
- [ ] `make check` passes

## Update 2026-09-16: the script is dead code — prefer deletion to repair

Measured on padme, after the ticket was filed:

- `scripts/claude-night-sweep.service` and `.timer`, which lines 94-95 copy into
  `/etc/systemd/system/`, **do not exist**. They were removed by 0882
  ("remove the nightbeat block", 90d56b4, 2026-09-09) — the same commit that
  last touched this script.
- No `claude-agent` user exists.
- No `/etc/claude-agent` directory exists.
- No `claude-night-sweep` timer is installed or listed.

So the script cannot run to completion: under `set -e` it aborts at the `cp` of
a missing unit file, having already created a system user, written
`/etc/claude-agent/env` and chmod'ed it. It provisions nothing and leaves
half-built state behind.

Nothing references it but its own test, and that test exercises only `--list`,
the side-effect-free entry point. It is green on a script whose provisioning
half is dead — an all-clear that cannot be told from "I could not look".

Recommendation: **delete `scripts/setup-claude-agent.sh` and
`tests/test_setup_claude_agent_projects.sh`** rather than teach the script to
resolve keys from the keystore. This follows the tooling-repo rule — prefer
deleting a guard and its tests over growing them — and removes one of the two
remaining `.env`-grep hazards outright. Reinstating an overnight agent later is
a fresh design against whatever secrets mechanism exists then, not a revival of
this file.

Deferred to the author: 0882 decommissioned the nightbeat, so confirming this
script is collateral of that decision, not an independent capability someone
still intends to use, is a judgment call outside the diff.

Consequence for the portability work: the materialization constraint collapses
to ONE consumer, `scripts/seat-runner.sh`, which is ours and patchable. R2R is
dormant and lives in another repo entirely (only project-memory references in
this tree). A general `with-secrets <provider> -- <cmd>` wrapper may not be
needed at all.
